E41031 false positive: 'derivative is lost' on no_diff out field with all-no_diff inputs
## Issue Description

`E41031` ("derivative is lost during assignment") fires in a `[Differentiable]` function when assigning to a field of a `no_diff out` parameter, even when every operand on the RHS is itself non-differentiable. There is no derivative flow into the assignment, so the diagnostic is a false positive and blocks valid code.

This was surfaced by falcor2 (`emissive_triangle_sampler_utils.slang`) after #11272 / #11273 unblocked an earlier `this_type` codegen error that previously aborted compilation before the auto-diff check ran. Reduced here to a 31-line standalone repro with no external dependencies.

## Reproducer Code

```slang
public struct DirSample
{
    public float3 pos_ws;
    public float distance;
    public float pdf;
}

[Differentiable]
public bool sample_dir(
    no_diff const float3 pos_ws,
    no_diff out DirSample sample,
    out float3 weight)
{
    sample = {};
    weight = float3(0);

    float3 to_light = sample.pos_ws - pos_ws;
    const float dist_sqr = max(1e-30f, dot(to_light, to_light));
    sample.distance = sqrt(dist_sqr);
    sample.pdf = dist_sqr / 1.0f;
    return true;
}

RWStructuredBuffer<float> out_buf;

[shader("compute")]
[numthreads(1, 1, 1)]
void main()
{
    DirSample s;
    float3 w;
    sample_dir(float3(0), s, w);
    out_buf[0] = s.distance + s.pdf;
}
```

## Compiler Command Line

```
slangc repro.slang -target hlsl -entry main -stage compute -o out.hlsl
```

## Expected Behavior

Clean compile. Every value feeding the assignments is `no_diff`:
- `pos_ws` parameter — declared `no_diff const float3`
- `sample.pos_ws` — read from a `no_diff out DirSample` parameter
- `to_light = sample.pos_ws - pos_ws` — both operands no_diff
- `dist_sqr = max(..., dot(to_light, to_light))` — entirely no_diff
- `sqrt(dist_sqr)`, `dist_sqr / 1.0f` — no_diff

There is no derivative to lose.

## Observed Behavior

```
error[E41031]: derivative is lost during assignment
  --> repro.slang:32:21
   |
32 | sample.distance = sqrt(dist_sqr);
   |                 ^ derivative is lost during assignment to non-differentiable
   |                   location, use 'detach()' to clarify intention.
---'
error[E41031]: derivative is lost during assignment
  --> repro.slang:33:16
   |
33 | sample.pdf = dist_sqr / 1.0f;
   |            ^ derivative is lost during assignment to non-differentiable
   |              location, use 'detach()' to clarify intention.
---'
```

## Where the Check Lives

`source/slang/slang-ir-check-differentiability.cpp:734` —
`Diagnostics::LossOfDerivativeAssigningToNonDifferentiableLocation`.

The guard is:

```cpp
if (carryNonTrivialDiffSet.contains(storeInst->getVal()) &&
    !canAddressHoldDerivative(diffTypeContext, storeInst->getPtr()))
{
    sink->diagnose(
        Diagnostics::LossOfDerivativeAssigningToNonDifferentiableLocation{
            .location = storeInst->sourceLoc,
        });
}
```

`canAddressHoldDerivative(...)` correctly reports `false` for the LHS (it is a `no_diff` parameter field). The defect is on the value side: `carryNonTrivialDiffSet` includes `sqrt(dist_sqr)` and `dist_sqr / 1.0f` even though `dist_sqr` is derived entirely from `no_diff` values. The set propagation in the auto-diff check is over-approximating.

## What I've Verified

- Reproduces with the repro above on current master (`18e72e1b6` + #11272 / #11277 fixes — neither touches auto-diff code; both PRs pass CI).
- The diagnostic itself (`LossOfDerivativeAssigningToNonDifferentiableLocation`, error code 41031) has been in the codebase since 2023.
- The auto-diff check was significantly refactored on 2026-04-01 in #9808 ("Refactor auto-diff implementation"). That refactor lands in 2026.9.1, but on 2026.9.1 falcor2 hit the `this_type` codegen error before the auto-diff check ran, so this regression went unnoticed there. A targeted bisect across #9808 would confirm whether the `carryNonTrivialDiffSet` propagation regressed there or earlier.
- Workarounds: wrapping each RHS in `detach()` (or marking `dist_sqr`/`to_light` with `no_diff`) silences the error, but is a band-aid — the diagnostic should not fire in the first place when no derivative flows in.

## Impact

Blocks falcor2's path-tracer module (`emissive_triangle_sampler_utils.slang`) on current master. With #11272 / #11273 / #11277 applied, this is the next-in-line error preventing falcor2 from using a current Slang.

